(i) The female gametophyte develops from the **megaspore mother cell (MMC)**, a single large cell with dense cytoplasm and a prominent nucleus, differentiated in the micropylar region of the nucellus. The sequence of changes:
- The MMC undergoes **meiotic division** to produce **four haploid megaspores** (a tetrad); this is megasporogenesis.
- In a majority of flowering plants **one megaspore is functional while the other three degenerate**.
- The nucleus of the functional megaspore divides mitotically to form two nuclei, which move to the opposite poles (the 2-nucleate embryo sac). Two more sequential mitotic divisions give the 4-nucleate and then the **8-nucleate** stages. These are **free nuclear divisions**: they are not followed immediately by cell wall formation.
- At the 8-nucleate stage, **three nuclei are at the micropylar end, three at the chalazal end and two in the centre**.
- After the 8-nucleate stage **cell walls are laid down**. The three cells at the micropylar end form the egg apparatus (two synergids and one egg cell), the three at the chalazal end are the antipodals, and the two **polar nuclei** lie in the large central cell below the egg apparatus.

So a typical angiosperm embryo sac at maturity is **8-nucleate and 7-celled**. Since it forms from a single megaspore, this is called monosporic development.

(ii) The diagram of the mature embryo sac is an oval sac with these labels (any four):
- **Antipodals**, three cells at the chalazal end;
- **Polar nuclei**, two nuclei in the centre;
- **Central cell**, the large cell holding the polar nuclei;
- **Egg** cell, at the micropylar end between the synergids;
- **Synergids**, two cells beside the egg;
- **Filiform apparatus**, the cellular thickenings at the micropylar tip of the synergids, which guide the pollen tube into the synergid.
